text:
mira: quick check — running into intermittent JSON parse exceptions in the java-netty SSE client after token refresh. chunks look fine but parser fails with partial-object errors. anyone seen this pattern?

jason: do you have an example of the chunk boundary that fails? sometimes utf8 multibyte splits or early 401s mid-stream cause it.

mira: here's a small repro chunk sequence we saw:
`...{\"choices\":[{\"text\":\"hel`
then next chunk `lo\"}]}...`
parser throws because it gets fed incomplete data mid-parse. we also observed a 401 logged right before the exception in some sessions (token refresh).

zoe: i ran into this last quarter. for java we switched to Jackson's non-blocking parser (createNonBlockingByteArrayParser) and feedInput() so the parser holds state across partial buffers instead of trying to reconstitute full objects. that + proper backpressure fixed most cases for us.

liam: for netty backpressure: disable autoRead when your inbound queue length > threshold and call channel.read() as you drain. basically maintain a bounded queue per connection. otherwise memory spikes and you get huge fragmented frames.

mira: thanks. i'm trying NonBlockingJsonParser — quick snippet i used:

```java
JsonFactory jf = new JsonFactory();
NonBlockingJsonParser p = (NonBlockingJsonParser) jf.createNonBlockingByteArrayParser();
// on each SSE data chunk (bytes)
p.feedInput(chunkBytes, 0, chunkLen);
JsonToken t;
while ((t = p.nextToken()) != null) {
  // handle tokens incrementally, collect events when complete
}
// when stream ends: p.endOfInput();
```

zoe: that looks right. also ensure you aren't handing the parser fragments after you already closed the stream on a 401. netty will fire the http status, then give you the body frames — if you close out before the parser sees endOfInput it errors. intercept status first.

mira: good call — our client currently applies new auth header after the SSE handshake in a handler which means we can get a 401 frame mid-stream. moving auth refresh earlier should prevent the server from injecting an auth error into an active event stream.

jason: small netty tips: 1) set channel.config().setAutoRead(false) in channelActive and use ctx.read() when queue drained, 2) use a small ArrayDeque<byte[]> as the inbound buffer and track bytesPending. backpressure = stop reading from socket when bytesPending > MAX_BYTES, 3) schedule a small executor to feed parser on the eventloop to avoid blocking netty threads while parsing.

engbot: deploy-bot posted: \"streaming-client-fix branch built and integration tests passed (3/4 flaky SSE tests rerun).\"

liam: if server-side NDJSON isn't an option, you can implement a lightweight framing detector locally: count open/close braces and only attempt to emit when braces balanced. it's brittle but works for simple payloads. prefer non-blocking parser though.

mira: implemented NonBlockingJsonParser + autoRead toggling + preflight token check. error rate dropped ~90% in staging. still seeing a couple of weird sessions where a reconnect returns a short 204 then server resumes frames — parser rejected that. adding a guard to treat non-200 preflight/reconnect as full stream termination.

zoe: nice. also add metrics: \"sse.partial_json_errors\", \"sse.auth_retries\", and \"sse.backpressure_paused_seconds\" so we can correlate.

mira: will add metrics + open a tiny PR with the parser change and netty handler tweaks. pinging @jason for review.

jason: :thumbsup: i'll review after standup, should be quick.

mira: thanks all — if anyone wants the failing sample chunks i can drop them in the PR comments.
